<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burkina Faso</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Burkina Faso is a landlocked country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, with its capital at Ouagadougou. Its central plateau was the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a> kingdoms, whose rulers still hold traditional authority, and the country was the French colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-volta">Upper Volta</a> before independence in 1960. It took its present name, meaning land of upright people, under Thomas Sankara in 1984.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burkina-faso.jpg" alt="Burkina Faso" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Drawn by SKopp, rewritten by Gabbe and Андрей Перцев · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Burkina_Faso.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Burkina Faso covers 274,223 square kilometres. Most of the country is a peneplain, a gently undulating landscape with a few isolated hills that are the last vestiges of a Precambrian massif. The southwest is a sandstone massif, bordered by cliffs up to 150 metres high, where the highest peak, Ténakourou, rises to 749 metres. The country owed its former name, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-volta">Upper Volta</a>, to three rivers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-volta">Black Volta</a> (Mouhoun), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-volta">White Volta</a> (Nakambé) and the Red Volta (Nazinon). Only the Black Volta and the Komoé flow year-round, and water shortages are often a problem in the north.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The northwest was populated by hunter-gatherers from about 14,000 BCE to 5,000 BCE, and agricultural settlements were established between 3600 and 2600 BCE. The oldest evidence of iron smelting in Burkina Faso dates from 800 to 700 BCE and forms part of the Ancient Ferrous Metallurgy World Heritage Site. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bura">Bura</a> culture existed in the southeast from the 3rd to the 13th century CE.</p><p>From medieval times until the end of the 19th century the central region was ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a>, descendants of Dagomba warriors from present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> who mixed with Mandé and other local peoples in the 11th century. They established separate kingdoms, among them Tenkodogo, Yatenga, Zandoma and Ouagadougou, and defended their territory, beliefs and social structure against attempts at conquest and conversion from Muslims to the northwest.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-sudan">Tarikh al-Sudan</a> reports that a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a> ruler took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> between 1328 and 1338, during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a>, and the Tarikh al-fattash describes conflicts between the 15th-century Mossi king Komdao and the Songhay ruler Sonni Ali. The Mossi were defeated at the Battle of Kobi in 1483. The French colonised the area in 1896 as part of French West Africa, and it became fully independent in 1960 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-volta">Republic of Upper Volta</a>. On 4 August 1984 President Thomas Sankara renamed it Burkina Faso.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ouagadougou is the capital and largest city. The name joins Burkina, from Mooré, meaning upright, with Faso, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangara">Dyula</a>, meaning fatherland. The country is a semi-presidential republic and a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>. About half the population is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a>, whose kingdom is led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mogho-naba">Mogho Naba</a>, whose court is in Ouagadougou. There are more than 60 indigenous languages, and Mooré is spoken by more than half the population. Most people are Muslim, with Christian and animist minorities. Literature rests on oral tradition, and Ouagadougou hosts the International Art and Craft Fair, one of the most important African handicraft fairs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Burkina Faso” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Despite their name, the tirailleurs sénégalais were recruited from across French West Africa, and many came from present-day Mali, <b>Burkina Faso</b> and Guinea rather than Senegal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…through his daughter Yennenga the Mossi kingdoms of Ouagadougou, Tenkodogo, Yatenga and Fada N'Grumah, whose people are nearly half of <b>Burkina Faso</b> and who honour her as their mother; the Bouna kingdom of Ivory Coast and the Dagaaba states of Ghana's Upper West also came out of Dagbon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dagbon">Kingdom of Dagbon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Beads of the first kind found at Igbo-Ukwu in southern Nigeria, at Gao and Essouk in Mali and at Kissi in <b>Burkina Faso</b> after the ninth century show the industry's part in regional trade, including the trans-Saharan trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ife">Kingdom of Ife · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Independence in the 1960s divided the Tuareg lands among Niger, Mali, Algeria, Libya and <b>Burkina Faso</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tuareg">Tuareg · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>…govern China, Vietnam, Laos and Cuba as one-party states, and North Korea has been led by three generations of the Kim family since 1948; Myanmar’s armed forces seized power in a coup in February 2021, and soldiers have taken power in Mali, <b>Burkina Faso</b> and Niger since 2020.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Africa saw a new wave, with coups in Sudan in 2019 and 2021, Mali in 2020 and 2021, Guinea in 2021, <b>Burkina Faso</b> in 2022, and Niger and Gabon in 2023.</blockquote><a href="/government/military-junta">Military junta · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
